# DESCRIPTION
# Using chocolate bar ratings, set up a hypothesis test for a sample mean difference.
# ENDDESCRIPTION

## DBsubject('WeBWorK')
## DBchapter('Nursing-Med Calc')
## DBsection('Problem')
## KEYWORDS('null hypothesis', 'alternative hypothesis', 'two-sample test')
## Author('Catherine Buell')
## Institution('Fitchburg State')

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros(
    "PGstandard.pl",   'PGML.pl',
    'randomPerson.pl', "parserPopUp.pl",
    "PGcourse.pl"
);

Context("Numeric")->flags->set(
    tolerance => 0.02,
    tolType   => "absolute",
);

@opts = ('\(<\)', '\(>\)', '\(=\)', '\(\neq\)');
$dd1  = DropDown([@opts], 2, useHTMLSelect => 0);
$dd2  = DropDown([@opts], 3, useHTMLSelect => 0);

$n = random(10, 20);

$mean_diff = random(0.20, 0.50, 0.05);

($p1, $p2) = randomPerson(n => 2);

BEGIN_PGML

[$p1] and [$p2] both really like chocolate, and their hobby is trying
different chocolate bars and giving them a rating on a 0-20 scale where 0
is worst and 20 is best. They suspect that [$p1]'s mean rating is different
from [$p2]'s, so they decide to test this. They try the same [$n] chocolate 
bars and each give them ratings. They find a sample mean difference 
([$p1] -[$p2]) of [$mean_diff].

a) The null hypothesis would be: [`H_0`]: [`\mu_{1}`] [_]{$dd1} [_]{Real(0)}

b) The alternative hypothesis would be: [`H_a`]:  [`\mu_{1}`] [_]{$dd2}  [_]{Real(0)}

c) What is [`n`]? [_]{$n}

d) What is [`\bar{x}`] (to two decimal places)? [_]{Real($mean_diff) }

END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
a) Since the mean [`\mu`] is interpreted as the difference between then, the null hypothesis is the neutral relationship and that is [`\mu=0`] (there is no difference in their ratings.)

b) Since [$p1] and [$p2] want to know if their ratings are different, the alternative
hypothesis is [`\mu \neq 0 `].

c) [`n`] is the number of samples  or [`[$n]`].

d) The sample mean difference is [`\bar{x} = [$mean_diff]`].
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
